DATA STRUCTURES – STUDENT SUMMARY NOTES
Key Concepts, Pseudocode, Algorithms and Quick Revision
Purpose: These notes are designed for quick student revision, classroom learning, laboratory preparation and examination preparation.
1. Data Structures
A data structure is a systematic way of organizing and storing data so that it can be accessed and processed efficiently.
Examples: Array, Linked List, Stack, Queue, Tree and Graph.
Classification:
•	Primitive: Integer, Character, Float, Pointer.
•	Non-Primitive – Linear: Array, Linked List, Stack, Queue.
•	Non-Primitive – Non-linear: Tree, Graph.
Importance:
•	Efficient storage and processing of data.
•	Faster searching, insertion and deletion.
•	Better memory utilization.
•	Makes complex problems easier to solve.
2. Data Structure Operations
Common operations are:
Operation	Meaning
Traversal	Visit each element
Insertion	Add a new element
Deletion	Remove an element
Searching	Find an element
Sorting	Arrange elements
Merging	Combine two data structures
Pseudocode – Array Traversal:
TRAVERSE(A, n)
1. Start
2. For i = 0 to n-1
       Print A[i]
3. Stop
Time Complexity: O(n)
3. Pointers
A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable.
int x = 10;
int *p;
p = &x;
Here, &x gives the address of x, p stores the address, and *p gives the value stored at that address.
Important operators:
Operator	Meaning
&	Address-of operator
*	Dereference operator
Applications: dynamic memory allocation, arrays, strings, linked lists, trees, structures and passing arguments by reference.
4. Dynamic Memory Allocation
Dynamic memory allocation means allocating memory during program execution, generally from the heap.
Function	Purpose
malloc()	Allocates memory; contents are uninitialized.
calloc()	Allocates memory and initializes it to zero.
realloc()	Changes the size of previously allocated memory.
free()	Releases dynamically allocated memory.
int *p;
p = (int *)malloc(5 * sizeof(int));

p = (int *)calloc(5, sizeof(int));

p = (int *)realloc(p, 10 * sizeof(int));

free(p);
Pseudocode – Dynamic Array:
DYNAMIC_ARRAY()
1. Start
2. Read n
3. Allocate memory for n integers
4. Read n elements
5. Display/process the elements
6. Release memory using free()
7. Stop
Important: Failure to release dynamically allocated memory can cause a memory leak.
5. Data Abstraction
Data abstraction means showing only the essential features of data and hiding unnecessary implementation details.
Example: In a stack, users work with push(), pop() and peek() without needing to know the internal implementation.
•	Reduces complexity.
•	Improves modularity.
•	Makes programs easier to maintain.
•	Separates interface from implementation.
6. Arrays
An array is a collection of elements of the same data type stored in contiguous memory locations.
Example: A = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
•	Advantage: direct/random access.
•	Advantage: simple traversal and implementation.
•	Limitation: static arrays have fixed size.
•	Insertion/deletion may require shifting elements.
7. Dynamically Allocated Arrays
A dynamic array allows its size to be decided at run time.
int n;
int *a;
scanf("%d", &n);
a = (int *)malloc(n * sizeof(int));
DYNAMIC_ARRAY()
1. Start
2. Read n
3. Allocate memory for n elements
4. For i = 0 to n-1
       Read A[i]
5. For i = 0 to n-1
       Display A[i]
6. Free allocated memory
7. Stop
8. Sparse Matrix
A sparse matrix is a matrix in which most elements are zero.
0  0  5
0  0  0
2  0  0
Triplet representation stores only non-zero elements. The first row commonly stores: number of rows, number of columns and number of non-zero elements.
3  3  2
0  2  5
2  0  2
Pseudocode – Sparse Matrix to Triplet:
SPARSE_TO_TRIPLET(A, rows, cols)
1. count = 0
2. For i = 0 to rows-1
3.   For j = 0 to cols-1
4.     If A[i][j] != 0
5.       count = count + 1
6. Store rows, cols, count
7. Scan A again
8. Store row, column and value for every non-zero element
9. Stop
Main benefit: saves memory when the matrix contains many zero values.
9. Polynomial Representation
A polynomial can be represented as a collection of terms, where each term contains a coefficient and an exponent.
Example: 5x³ + 4x² + 2x + 7
Coefficient	Exponent
5	3
4	2
2	1
7	0
struct Term
{
    int coefficient;
    int exponent;
};
Polynomial addition logic:
Compare exponents.
If exponent1 == exponent2:
    Add coefficients.
If exponent1 > exponent2:
    Copy first term.
If exponent2 > exponent1:
    Copy second term.
Continue until all terms are processed.
Example: (5x² + 3x + 2) + (4x² + 2x + 5) = 9x² + 5x + 7
