11. Naive Pattern Searching
Naive pattern searching compares the pattern with the text at every possible starting position.
Example: Text = ABABCABAB, Pattern = ABAB.
NAIVE_SEARCH(T, P)
1. n = length(T)
2. m = length(P)
3. For i = 0 to n-m
4.     j = 0
5.     While j < m AND T[i+j] == P[j]
6.         j = j + 1
7.     If j == m
8.         Print "Pattern found at", i
9. Stop
Worst-case time complexity: O(n × m).
12. KMP Algorithm
KMP (Knuth-Morris-Pratt) improves pattern searching by using an LPS array.
LPS = Longest Proper Prefix which is also a Suffix.
For Pattern = ABAB, LPS = [0, 0, 1, 2].
KMP_SEARCH(T, P)
1. Construct LPS array for P
2. i = 0, j = 0
3. While i < length(T)
4.     If T[i] == P[j]
5.         i = i + 1
6.         j = j + 1
7.     If j == length(P)
8.         Pattern found at i-j
9.         j = LPS[j-1]
10.    Else if T[i] != P[j]
11.        If j != 0
12.            j = LPS[j-1]
13.        Else
14.            i = i + 1
15. Stop
Complexity: O(n + m). KMP avoids repeated comparisons by using information already obtained from the pattern.
13. Insertion Sort
Insertion Sort builds the sorted array one element at a time by inserting each new element into its correct position.
INSERTION_SORT(A, n)
1. For i = 1 to n-1
2.     key = A[i]
3.     j = i - 1
4.     While j >= 0 AND A[j] > key
5.         A[j+1] = A[j]
6.         j = j - 1
7.     A[j+1] = key
8. Stop
Case	Time Complexity
Best	O(n)
Average	O(n²)
Worst	O(n²)
Characteristics: simple, stable, in-place, and useful for small or nearly sorted data.
14. Radix Sort
Radix Sort is a non-comparison sorting algorithm that sorts numbers digit by digit.
Typical order: units digit → tens digit → hundreds digit → and so on.
RADIX_SORT(A, n)
1. Find maximum element
2. exp = 1
3. While maximum / exp > 0
4.     Perform stable counting sort according to:
       digit = (A[i] / exp) % 10
5.     exp = exp * 10
6. Stop
Time complexity: O(d × (n + k)), where d is the number of digits, n is the number of elements and k is the digit range (normally 10).
